								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=850.msg8252#msg8252">Re: Conviction...no loyalty to their guildees</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #13 on:</b> June 04, 2004, 03:48:09 pm &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=850.msg8250#msg8250">Quote from: Kelce on June 04, 2004, 03:31:30 pm</a></div><div class="quote"><br /><br />&nbsp;risking a rookie when a veteran is available? <br /></div><br />Articstorm is not a rookie by any means. But once again I point out, winning the bragging rights of conquering this event seemed more important than chemistry and loyalty. And this is a practice and ideaology not limited to Conviction even tho this particular incident happens to be.<br /><br />Not implying that I do not value the time of other persons on the raid, nor am I implying that I do not see the points of your arguments. Hell, I&#039;m not even arguing against your opinion. Just pointing out my personal view. I have to go with my guild everytime, and trust them to do it right the first time or learn from the failure. <br /><br />Although the practice of just poaching and leaching success from other people who have done it before and then falsley claiming success through your own works seems to be working for some.&nbsp; <img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Smileys/Rodcet/cool.gif" alt="Cool" border="0" /><br />
						</td>
